<!--
    GH-839 (kuna): the AArch64 guard for `option reloadarg`.
    clang 14 -O2 for aarch64-linux-gnu of
      void fa1(void){ use(__builtin_frame_address(1)); }
      void fa2(int k){ printf("%d %p\n", k, __builtin_frame_address(1)); }
      void fa3(void){ use3(__builtin_frame_address(0),
                           __builtin_frame_address(1), __builtin_return_address(0)); }
      void fa4(void){ void *p = __builtin_frame_address(1); use(0); use(p); }
    relocated flat at 0x100000 (the format string at 0x100100; use, printf and
    use3 are symbols with no bytes).  Each function reads the caller's frame
    pointer with ldr xN,[x29], a LOAD from the frame whose slot holds the
    incoming x29.  x29 is callee-saved, so the value is real and both passes
    (reloadarg on, then off) must pass it.
-->
